.lua
book.chapter(14, "Patterns, guards, and total functions")
.endlua
.sh 2 "A match is structured case analysis"
.pp
A match evaluates its scrutinee, then considers alternatives in source order.
Each branch begins with a vertical bar, followed by a pattern, an optional
guard, an arrow, and a body. Patterns both test structure and introduce
bindings. They let a function describe the domain's cases directly instead
of manually checking tags and extracting fields through unchecked indices.
The wildcard underscore ignores a value without giving it a useful name.
.lua
book.code("metaml", [=[describe xs := match xs with
  | [] -> "empty"
  | x :: _ when x < 0 -> "starts negative"
  | _ :: _ -> "nonempty"
Test.equal "empty case" (describe []) "empty"
Test.equal "guarded case" (describe [~2, 3]) "starts negative"
Test.equal "fallback case" (describe [2, 3]) "nonempty"]=], "Shape tests followed by a guard")
.endlua
.pp
Supported patterns include primitive literals, unit, variables, tuples, fixed
lists, cons cells, constructors, records, and annotations. Each form encodes a
different structural requirement. A fixed list pattern states a particular
length; a cons pattern states only that at least one element exists and binds
the rest. Choose the weakest pattern that captures the invariant needed by
the branch, rather than accidentally rejecting longer valid input.
.sh 2 "Selection controls evaluation"
.pp
The scrutinee is evaluated once. A branch's bindings become available only
after its pattern matches. The guard then runs in that extended environment;
if it is false, matching continues with later branches. Only the selected
branch body is evaluated. This makes match an appropriate way to delay a
fallback computation until absence or failure is known, unlike an ordinary
helper whose default argument is evaluated eagerly.
.lua
book.code("metaml", [=[value_or_compute option fallback := match option with
  | Some value -> value
  | None -> fallback ()
Test.equal "unused fallback" (value_or_compute (Some 7) (fn _ -> 1 / 0)) 7
Test.equal "used fallback" (value_or_compute None (fn _ -> 42)) 42]=],
    "A function-valued fallback is invoked only in the selected branch")
book.algorithm("Select a match branch", {
    "Evaluate the scrutinee once to a value v.",
    "For each branch, in source order:",
    {1, "Attempt its structural pattern against v."},
    {1, "On success, extend the environment with pattern bindings."},
    {1, "If a guard exists and is false, continue to the next branch."},
    {1, "Evaluate and return this branch's body."},
    "If no branch is selected, report M0401."
})
.endlua
.pp
Effects in guards are therefore observable even when the branch body is not
selected. An effectful first guard can run before a later successful branch.
Pure predicates make case analysis easier to reason about and refactor. If
an operation needs to log every attempted branch, that is a procedural
contract worth documenting explicitly rather than hiding inside what appears
to be a declarative pattern match.
.sh 2 "Order is part of meaning"
.pp
Place specific cases before catch-all cases. A wildcard branch first makes
later branches unreachable at runtime even if the checker does not reject
every such arrangement. Guards also affect coverage: a pattern matching all
integers with a positive-value guard does not cover zero or negative values.
The pattern's structural breadth is not the same as the guarded branch's
effective domain.
.lua
book.code("metaml", [=[classify n := match n with
  | 0 -> "zero"
  | x when x < 0 -> "negative"
  | _ -> "positive"
Test.equal "literal priority" (classify 0) "zero"
Test.equal "guard priority" (classify (~1)) "negative"
Test.equal "remaining domain" (classify 3) "positive"]=], "A deliberate partition of integer inputs")
.endlua
.pp
In a tree interpreter, order can distinguish specialized optimizations from
general traversal. A branch for adding two numeric literals should precede
a general addition branch if both occur in one match. In a validator, however,
an overly broad fallback can mask a newly added constructor. Whenever a domain
model changes, review the matches that interpret it, not just the declaration
that introduces the new case.
.sh 2 "Totality is a contract you must establish"
.pp
A total function returns a defined result for every input in its stated
domain. Successful type checking does not prove every nested match and guard
is exhaustive in this implementation. If no alternative matches, evaluation
reports M0401. Function parameter patterns use the same matching machinery,
so a parameter such as x :: xs is a partial interface unless callers guarantee
a nonempty list.
.lua
book.code("metaml-error:M0401", [=[head_only (x :: _) := x
head_only []]=], "A partial parameter pattern fails at application")
.endlua
.pp
For public APIs, represent ordinary absence with Option and expected failures
with Result. That makes the missing case part of the return type rather than
an undocumented evaluator exception. A parser returning Err can participate
in a validation pipeline; a deliberately failed parameter pattern aborts the
current computation. The two behaviors are not interchangeable merely because
both arise from an input that the caller did not expect.
.sh 2 "Test the partition, not just one branch"
.pp
A useful match test suite contains a witness for each reachable branch and
for each relevant boundary between guards. For a sign classifier, test zero,
one positive, and one negative value. For a list shape classifier, test empty,
singleton, and longer lists. For a recursive tree, test a leaf and a node
whose children exercise different cases. These tests correspond to the
structure of the domain rather than mirroring every implementation line.
.pp
Property-style tests can add confidence. If a classifier returns one of three
documented labels, verify that membership for a finite generated sample.
If a transformation preserves evaluation, compare the original and transformed
tree over representative inputs. Such tests remain useful when the branch
implementation changes because they state a semantic relationship instead
of a particular control-flow arrangement.
.sh 2 "Sources and further work"
.pp
Read the pattern representation and matching routines in MetaML.hpp and
MetaML.d, plus the Option and Result library definitions. Write a safe head
function returning Option, then a function that consumes its result with a
lazy fallback. Add a guard whose boundary is zero and test both adjacent
values. Finally, explain why a pattern generator in a comprehension skips a
mismatch while a function parameter pattern reports M0401; they serve different
control-flow contracts despite sharing structural matching concepts.
